<article xml:id="Submission" label="Submission">
  <title>Chapter 3-1 Submission</title>

  <section>
    <title>3-1-2 Proof</title>

    <p>
      By Definition, parallel lines do not intersect.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <image source="asset-3-1-2-image.png">
<shortdescription>Image required for the proof of Theorem 3-1-2.</shortdescription>
</image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Suppose <m>m(\angle a)=m(\angle c)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose that <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m> intersect to the left of our
      transversal. Then we have a triangle, and by the Exterior Angle Theorem,
      <m>m(\angle a)&lt;m(\angle c)</m>. <m>\rightarrow\!\leftarrow</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      As a similar argument applies to intersections on the right, the theorem
      is proven.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
</pretext>